*/

/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:25] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: true
# space: desk

= Synopsis: Social media ad montage → "the internet says anyone can do it" → Vee tries → something comes out → "...what is this?"

INT. VEE'S DESK - DAY

= Visual: Quick montage — fake social media ads flash across screen. "Build an app in 60 seconds." "No code needed." "AI does everything." Bold text, sparkly effects. TikTok-style swipe transitions.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Build an app with one prompt. No code. No experience. Just vibes. The internet says anyone can do it.

= Visual: More ads — a smiling influencer pointing at a laptop. "I made ten thousand dollars with one prompt." A countdown timer: "60 seconds to your first app."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every ad. Every thread. Every video. The message is the same: just type what you want, and AI will build it.

= Visual: Desk scene — Vee's hands type a prompt on keyboard. She looks excited. Hits enter. Screen glows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Vee believed it. She typed a prompt. Hit enter.

= Visual: Something appears on screen. Result loads — colorful but chaotic. A website, technically.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something came out. But...

/* vee_reaction: 1/10 */
= Vee squints — confused expression. Bee peeks from behind monitor, antennae flat. 1.5 seconds.

/* SEGMENT 2: MISCONCEPTION [0:25-1:20] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: screen

= Synopsis: "Make me a store" → wrong → "be more specific" → worse → "try different tools" → same problem

INT. SCREEN SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She typed: "Make me an online store."

= Visual: AI generates a website. It loads. It exists. It's... technically a website.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And something appeared. A website. With a login button that goes to the shopping cart. A checkout page in neon pink and lime green. And a search bar that searches... nothing.

= Visual: Close-up of the website — login button labeled "Cart," garish color scheme, empty search results. Each problem highlighted with a subtle red pulse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It didn't crash. It didn't throw an error. It just... wasn't what she wanted.

/* vee_reaction: 2/10 */
= Vee frozen expression — not angry, not sad. Just... "what?" 1.5 seconds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So she got more specific. A blue header. Categories on the left. Cart icon top right. Modern font. Responsive layout. Autocomplete search.

= Visual: Prompt grows on screen. Camera slowly zooms out — text extends beyond the screen. Tiny font. Endless instructions.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The result? Worse. More detail made it more confused. Because detail is not direction.

/* vee_reaction: 3/10 */
= Vee slumps back in chair. Hands drop from keyboard. She looks at Bee — Bee shrugs with tiny arms. 1.5 seconds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She switched tools. Three different AI assistants. Same prompt. Three different wrong answers.

= Visual: Three browser windows open side by side. Three different tools. Three different results. All wrong in different ways.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The problem wasn't the tool. It was something else entirely.

/* SEGMENT 3: THE_CRACK [1:20-2:20] */
# visual_mode: C
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: true
# space: screen

= Synopsis: Same prompt three runs → three different results → "AI predicts, not understands" → cooking metaphor intro → "she never checked the recipe"

INT. SCREEN SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same prompt. Same tool. Three times in a row.

= Visual: Screen splits into three panels. Each shows a different result. One purple-themed, one text-only, one mostly images. Completely different layouts.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three completely different stores. Same exact prompt. Why? Because the AI isn't reading your prompt and understanding it. It's predicting the next word. Every time, slightly different. Like rolling dice with language.

= Visual: Zoom into one screen — text appearing letter by letter, each letter a probability cloud, a dice roll. Not "understanding" — predicting.

/* vee_reaction: 4/10 */
= Vee looks left, right, left — head swiveling between the three screens. 1.5 seconds.

/* extended_metaphor_start */

= Visual: Transition — screen space dissolves. A robot wearing a chef's hat stands in a kitchen. Industrial steel, professional setup. No recipe in sight.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Think of it like cooking. You walk into a restaurant and say: "Make me dinner." The chef starts cooking immediately.

= Visual: Robot chef grabs random ingredients. Chops, fries, plates something. Puts it on the counter.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something appears on the plate. It's food. Technically. But it's not what you wanted, because you never said what you wanted.

= Visual: The plate sits on the counter. A fork pokes it. Confused reaction from a small figure silhouette.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The AI wrote the recipe. She tasted the dish. But she never checked the recipe.

/* SEGMENT 4: CORE [2:20-5:00] */
# visual_mode: B
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: whiteboard

= Synopsis: Cooking metaphor continues (detail vs intent → recipe card → customer question) → Karpathy definition → good/bad at → tool montage (15s) → level spectrum

INT. WHITEBOARD SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you try again. "Something Italian. Pasta. Creamy." The chef gets excited. Four types of cheese. Pasta swimming in sauce. Garlic bread stuffed inside the pasta somehow.

= Visual: The result is a mountain of food. Technically Italian. Technically creamy. Also completely inedible.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
More detail. Same problem. Because you gave ingredients, not intent.

= Visual: Transition — a hand slides a small card across the counter. Recipe card — simple, three lines.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now imagine one card. "Pasta carbonara. Four ingredients. No cream. Serves two."

= Visual: Robot chef follows the recipe. Controlled. Precise. Plating is clean. Result looks like actual carbonara.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same chef. Same kitchen. Completely different result.

= Visual: Side-by-side — left: the chaotic cream mountain, right: clean carbonara.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But here's the question nobody asks. Would you serve this to a customer?

= Visual: The carbonara plate slides toward a dining table. A real person sits there. Menu, napkin, wine glass.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Your friend's kitchen? Sure, experiment. A restaurant with paying customers? You'd want to taste it first. Check the seasoning. Make sure it doesn't make someone sick.

= Visual: A health inspector appears with a clipboard. The robot chef nervously holds up the plate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
AI is that chef. Fast, capable, ready to go. But without a recipe, it improvises. When you're cooking for yourself, improvisation is fine. When you're cooking for others — you need more than vibes.

/* extended_metaphor_end */

= Visual: Transition — whiteboard clears. New text appears, building word by word.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In February twenty twenty-five, Andrej Karpathy — one of the founders of OpenAI — gave this a name. Vibe coding. Quote: "You just talk in natural language, run it, and most of the time it just works."

= Visual: Karpathy quote appears on whiteboard, styled like a citation. Clean typography.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But here's the word most people skip. "With." Vibe coding isn't AI coding for you. It's you coding with AI. The decisions — what to build, what's good enough, what's wrong — those are yours. The implementation — that's the AI's job.

= Visual: Two-column diagram. Left: "YOU" — decide, judge, direct. Right: "AI" — write, execute, generate. Arrow between them: "collaboration."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Not outsourcing. Collaboration. And fourteen months after Karpathy named it, a quarter of Y Combinator's latest batch shipped products with ninety-five percent AI-generated code. This isn't a prediction. It's already happening.

= Visual: YC logo + "95% AI-generated" stat appears. Bar chart showing rapid adoption curve.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So what can AI actually do well? Prototypes — ten minutes, you have something to react to. Scripts and automations. Learning tools — "explain this code," "show me an example."

= Visual: Three panels — a prototype mockup, a terminal running a script, a chat explaining code. Each with a green checkmark.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What can't it do without supervision? Production systems. Security. Payment processing — where a bug means someone gets charged twice.

= Visual: Three panels — a server with a warning sign, a lock with a crack, a credit card with an error. Each with a red X.

= Visual: Transition — five tool logos flash in quick succession, 2 seconds each.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Cursor, Claude Code, Replit, Kiro, Windsurf — five tools, same idea. Different interfaces. Same principle. You decide what. AI decides how.

# shorts_candidate: true

= Visual: All five tool logos shrink into a row. Above them: "YOU decide WHAT. AI decides HOW."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And there's a spectrum. Level one: copy-pasting from ChatGPT. Level two: an AI IDE like Cursor. Level three: an autonomous agent with a spec. Wherever you start is fine. But know where you are.

= Visual: Three-step staircase diagram. L1 (bottom) → L2 (middle) → L3 (top). Each labeled.

/* SEGMENT 5: REFRAME [5:00-5:55] */
# visual_mode: B
# characters: []
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: whiteboard

= Synopsis: "Not an easy button — a new skill" → data anchor → "Judgment is the hardest part" → EP02 bridge

INT. WHITEBOARD SPACE - CONTINUOUS

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So here's the reframe. Vibe coding is not an easy button. It's a new skill — like learning to drive. The car does the moving. You do the steering.

= Visual: Whiteboard — "easy button" icon gets crossed out. Replaced by a steering wheel with two hands on it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And the hardest part isn't the typing. It's the judgment. Looking at what the AI made and knowing — is this good enough? Is this safe? Would I bet money on this working tomorrow?

= Visual: A scale appears. One side: "good enough?" Other side: "dangerous?" The scale tips back and forth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Professional developers do this every day. They review code. They test it. They ask: "what could go wrong?" That's not a coding skill. That's a thinking skill. And you already have it.

= Visual: A non-developer looking at a plate of food — "would I serve this?" Same judgment, different domain.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The starting point — the thing that makes all of this work — is knowing what you're building. Not how. What. That's where episode two picks up.

= Visual: A question mark floats: "What are you building?" — left unanswered. EP02 tease.

/* SEGMENT 6: OUTRO_CTA [5:55-6:30] */
# visual_mode: A
# characters: vee
# shorts_candidate: false
# space: desk

= Synopsis: Three concrete actions → install, prompt, write the gap → that gap is EP02

INT. VEE'S DESK - DAY

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's what you do today. Three things.

= Visual: Desk scene — a sticky note appears with "1, 2, 3" written on it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One — pick a tool. Cursor, Replit, Claude Code. Install it.

= Visual: Download progress bar fills. App opens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Two — type one prompt. "Make me a to-do app." Anything.

= Visual: Prompt typed. Enter pressed. Something appears on screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three — before you close the tab, write down what you wanted, what you got, and the gap between them.

= Visual: Vee looks at the result. She picks up a pen. Writes on a sticky note — three words in three rows.

/* vee_reaction: 5/10 */
= Vee looks at the result. Head tilt. Then — a small smile. Not because it's good. Because she's starting. 1.5 seconds.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That gap — between what you imagined and what appeared — is where it all begins. Next time, we turn that gap into the most powerful tool in vibe coding.

= Visual: The sticky note gets pinned next to the monitor. On it: "Wanted: ___ / Got: ___ / Gap: ___". Glowing warm amber.

/* vee_reaction: 6/10 */
= Vee smiles — small, knowing. Bee does a tiny loop in the air. Fade out. 1.5 seconds.

> END OF EPISODE 01
